cartridge "COMET MINER/AMY & CLAUDE/2026"

' COMET MINER - an original game by Amy & Claude for Amy Studio, 2026, in Amy.
'
' A small mining ship orbits a spinning comet. Crystals (pink) sit in the
' comet's rim and turn with it. Fly low over one and drill it with the beam.
'
' Controls (either controller, joystick + side buttons; no keypad):
'   LEFT / RIGHT   thrust along the orbit (counter-clockwise / clockwise);
'                  the ship keeps its speed (inertia) and slows by itself
'   UP / DOWN      climb away from the comet / dive toward it
'   BUTTON         drill beam, straight down to the comet (0.25 s). A
'                  crystal under the beam's tip is mined. A beam that mines
'                  nothing breaks the chain and the drill cools down 0.75 s.
'                  While drilling, the ship holds still (no thrust).
' Goal: mine the crystals shown at the bottom left (6 on the first comet,
' one more each comet, at most 12) before the air bar runs out.
' Score: a crystal is 100 x chain (chain = crystals mined in a row without a
' miss, 1..5); the air left at COMET CLEAR is a bonus (10 a second).
' Hazards: rocks work loose (they wobble on the rim for 2/3 s), then fly off
' the spinning rim along it; vents blink for 1 s, then jet ice straight out;
' from comet 2 the solar wind blows (its arrow blinks 1.5 s first) and
' pushes the ship - into the comet if you let it; from comet 3 the spin
' reverses now and then (the spin arrow blinks first). A hit, a crash or
' running out of air costs a ship; 3 ships, +1 every third comet (max 5).
'
' Screen: HUD rows 1 and 22, play rows 2-21, columns 2-29 (CRT safe area).
' VDP: Graphics II, three separate pattern AND colour thirds (R3 = $FF).
' The comet is a 64 x 64 pixel bitmap (columns 12-19, rows 8-15 = the middle
' third) drawn into tile patterns, double-buffered: codes 128-191 (A) and
' 192-255 (B) of the middle third. Every 4 frames the hidden buffer is
' cleared and redrawn and the names flip in the vertical blank (0 frames
' of a half-drawn comet on screen). Drawing: 128 outline samples (radius
' profile Prof, 2 angle units apart), 4 craters, 3 vents, 3 crystals; each
' point is a polar lookup (RsTab: radius-scaled quarter-wave sine) and a
' read-modify-write of one VRAM byte (pixels in the same byte merged).
' Trigonometry: angles are 8 bits (256 a turn); every sin/cos comes from
' quarter-wave tables (SinQ for unit vectors, RsTab for r sin q, r = 0..71).
' No multiply anywhere in the frame (Amy has none of its own here either).
' Positions and speeds are 8.8 fixed point (ship angle Th, angular speed Om,
' altitude AltF and its speed AltV, comet angle AngF and spin SpinV, debris
' X, Y, VX, VY).
' Sprites (16 x 16): slot 0 the ship (or its explosion), slot 1 the beam or
' the thrust flame, slots 2-3 the hazards (debris / ice jets: two at most).
' Four sprites in all, so never more than 4 on a line and never flicker.

